# Supervised Resources

## Overview

Your Plugin needs a process: a cache, a connection pool, a rate limiter. If
an Action starts it, nothing owns it, and it outlives the Agent. Declare it
with `child_spec/1` instead. The Agent Server starts it under the Agent's
activation supervisor, waits until it's ready, passes its PID to your Plugin
callbacks, and restarts it together with the Server.

In this Livebook you'll build a deduplication Plugin whose runtime refuses
repeated Signal IDs:

```text
first Signal    -> commits %{messages: ["hello"], accepted: 1}
same Signal ID  -> {:error, :duplicate_signal}
```

Then you'll kill the runtime and watch the activation recover. Run the
cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## The Callbacks

| Callback | Invoked | Receives |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `child_spec/1` | When the activation starts, recovers, or replaces the runtime | A `Jido.Plugin.Init` value |
| `await_ready/2` | After every runtime root starts, in declaration order, before the first Turn | The runtime PID |
| `admit/3` | Before each live Turn | The runtime PID first |
| `after_commit/3` | After each commit, before Directives | The runtime PID first |
| `dispatch/4`, `prepare_dispatch/4` | After commit | The runtime PID first |

## Define the Runtime

The runtime is a plain GenServer. Its `start_link/1` receives a
`Jido.Plugin.Init` with the Agent ID, the Plugin's committed state value, the
revision (`state_version`), the Jido instance, and the Plugin options.

This one registers in an application Registry so the notebook can find it:

```elixir
{:ok, _registry} = Registry.start_link(keys: :unique, name: GuidePlugins.Registry)
```

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime do
  use GenServer

  alias Jido.Plugin.Init

  def start_link(%Init{} = init) do
    name = {:via, Registry, {GuidePlugins.Registry, {:dedupe, init.agent_id}}}
    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, init, name: name)
  end

  def whereis(agent_id) do
    case Registry.lookup(GuidePlugins.Registry, {:dedupe, agent_id}) do
      [{pid, _}] -> pid
      [] -> nil
    end
  end

  def info(runtime), do: GenServer.call(runtime, :info)

  @impl true
  def init(%Init{} = init) do
    {:ok,
     %{
       seen: MapSet.new(),
       accepted: init.plugin_state,
       revision: init.state_version,
       commits: 0
     }}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:ready?, _from, state), do: {:reply, :ok, state}

  def handle_call(:info, _from, state),
    do: {:reply, %{state | seen: MapSet.size(state.seen)}, state}

  def handle_call({:claim, signal_id}, _from, state) do
    if MapSet.member?(state.seen, signal_id),
      do: {:reply, {:error, :duplicate_signal}, state},
      else: {:reply, :ok, %{state | seen: MapSet.put(state.seen, signal_id)}}
  end

  def handle_call({:commit, commit}, _from, state) do
    {:reply, :ok,
     %{
       state
       | accepted: commit.plugin_state,
         revision: commit.state_version,
         commits: state.commits + 1
     }}
  end
end
```

`init/1` builds only from the committed value and revision in
`Jido.Plugin.Init`. Every runtime generation starts from a known commit.

## Define the Plugin

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Dedupe do
  use Jido.Plugin

  alias GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts),
    do: {:accepted, Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(0) |> Zoi.default(0)}

  @impl true
  def reduce(reduction, _opts), do: {:ok, reduction.plugin_state + 1}

  @impl true
  def child_spec(init), do: Supervisor.child_spec({Runtime, init}, id: __MODULE__)

  @impl true
  def await_ready(runtime, _opts), do: GenServer.call(runtime, :ready?)

  @impl true
  def admit(runtime, admission, _opts) do
    with :ok <- GenServer.call(runtime, {:claim, admission.signal.id}),
         do: {:ok, nil}
  end

  @impl true
  def after_commit(runtime, commit, _opts),
    do: GenServer.call(runtime, {:commit, commit})
end
```

Rules for the child spec:

- Use `:permanent` restart, the `Supervisor.child_spec/2` default. With any
  other restart type, the activation stops during startup with reason
  `:invalid_plugin_child_spec`.
- Return quickly from `start_link/1`. Do slow work, such as opening a
  connection, behind `await_ready/2`.

## Define the Agent

This notebook attaches the Plugin with `extensions:` because a Livebook cell
has no source file for `plugin` discovery. In application code, write
`plugin GuidePlugins.Dedupe` in the `agent` block.

```elixir
defmodule GuidePlugins.Inbox do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "inbox",
    extensions: [GuidePlugins.Dedupe]

  agent do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{messages: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/supervised-resources")

    route "inbox.receive", as: :receive do
      action %{text: text},
        name: "receive",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | messages: context.agent_state.messages ++ [text]}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Start and Wait

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, inbox} = Jido.start_agent(GuidePlugins.Inbox, id: "inbox-1")

Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(inbox)
```

The result is `:ok`.

Until every `await_ready/2` returns `:ok`, reads work and Turns return
`{:error, :not_ready}`. `readiness_timeout:` limits the whole wait. The
default is 5,000 ms. A caller timeout on `await_ready/2` doesn't cancel
startup.

## Refuse a Repeat

Send the same Signal twice:

```elixir
signal = GuidePlugins.Inbox.receive_signal!(%{text: "hello"})

{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, signal)
repeat = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, signal)

{agent.state, repeat}
```

The result is `{%{messages: ["hello"], accepted: 1}, {:error, :duplicate_signal}}`.
The second call has the same Signal ID, so the runtime refused it in
`admit/3`.

## Read the Runtime

```elixir
runtime = GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime.whereis("inbox-1")
GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime.info(runtime)
```

The result is `%{seen: 1, accepted: 1, revision: 1, commits: 1}`.

`after_commit/3` delivered that commit as a `Jido.AgentServer.Plugin.Commit`:
the Plugin's committed value, the revision, and identity fields. It has no
complete Agent and no way to change state.

Hooks run after every commit, in declaration order, before any Directive.
`commit_timeout:` limits the whole hook batch. The default is 5,000 ms. A
failed hook skips later hooks and all Directives. The commit and the reply
to the caller stay intact.

## Crash the Runtime

Kill the runtime and monitor the Server:

```elixir
server_ref = Process.monitor(inbox)
Process.exit(runtime, :kill)

receive do
  {:DOWN, ^server_ref, :process, _pid, reason} -> reason
after
  1_000 -> :still_running
end
```

The result is `:shutdown`. The activation supervisor uses `:one_for_all`, so
losing a required runtime also stops the Server. Then it restarts both.
Wait for the new Server:

```elixir
new_inbox =
  Enum.find_value(1..50, fn _ ->
    Process.sleep(20)

    case Jido.whereis_agent("inbox-1") do
      pid when is_pid(pid) and pid != inbox -> pid
      _ -> nil
    end
  end)

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(new_inbox)
runtime = GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime.whereis("inbox-1")

{Jido.AgentServer.agent(new_inbox).state, GuidePlugins.Dedupe.Runtime.info(runtime)}
```

The result is
`{%{messages: ["hello"], accepted: 1}, %{seen: 0, accepted: 1, revision: 1, commits: 0}}`.

The committed state survived: the Server recovered from its activation
checkpoint. The new runtime started from that commit (`accepted: 1`,
`revision: 1`). The `seen` set is empty because it lived only in the old
process.

## Memory Isn't State

Send the old Signal again:

```elixir
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(new_inbox, signal)
agent.state
```

The result is `%{messages: ["hello", "hello"], accepted: 2}`. The duplicate
got through.

Two rules follow:

- If the runtime must remember something across restarts, store it in the
  Plugin's owned state and rebuild it from `Jido.Plugin.Init`.
- If a running resource needs a later committed value, read it with
  `Jido.Plugin.state/2`. Don't cache the complete Agent.

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("inbox-1")
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- `child_spec/1` declares one `:permanent` runtime root. It receives a
  `Jido.Plugin.Init`.
- The Server starts every root, then blocks Turns until each `await_ready/2`
  returns `:ok`.
- Runtime callbacks receive the runtime PID as their first argument.
- `after_commit/3` sees each committed owned value and revision before
  Directives run.
- The Server and its runtime roots fail and restart together.
- A new runtime rebuilds from committed state. In-memory data is lost.

